{"qid":"Q2295939","names":["Duchy of Gascony"],"title":"Duchy of Gascony","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Duchy of Gascony (Vasconia) was a territory of southwestern France south of the Garonne, shaped around its Basque population whose repeated revolts against Frankish overlordship defined its early politics; under Charlemagne and his successors the Carolingians appointed counts to undermine ducal authority while the duchy oscillated between vassalage and autonomy. From 864 a native ducal line founded by Sancho (Sans) Mitarra ruled with only loose ties to the Frankish kings, and over the following century power devolved to constituent counties such as Béarn, Armagnac, and Bigorre. The autonomous Gascon duchy persisted until, after the line of its dukes ended in the early 11th century, it was inherited and absorbed by the Dukes of Aquitaine around 1053.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The duchy's political outline is well-documented in Carolingian and Capetian chronicles, but the precise territorial boundaries — particularly in the Pyrenean borderlands — shift considerably across the period and are reconstructed from narrative rather than cadastral sources.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Duchy of Gascony","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Lop II","nativeName":"Lop II","roman":"Lupus II","start":770,"end":778,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Sans I Lop","nativeName":"Sans I Lop","roman":"Sancho I","start":778,"end":812,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Seguin I Lop","nativeName":"Seguin I Lop","